{# {% call tufte.two_col('32%', side='right') %}{% include "includes/cek-anim.md" %}{% endcall %} #}

The `compose.mk` language is called **CMK**, a.k.a **cmk-lang**, or simply 'cmk'.

Before the elevator pitch; first the superlatives.  Things about CMK that are, as far as I know, completely unique:

1. First and only actually useful [matrioshka language]({{_}}/matrioshka).
1. The only non-research language built on [mobile ambients]({{_}}/cmk/banana#banana-ambient).
1. The one and only language that compiles to `Makefile`, and hijacks `make` as a gadget[^weird-machine].

The simplest way to tell the rest of the story is a great deal for you on elevator pitches, 3 for the price of 1.

{{ tufte.two_col_clear() }}

> **Tier-1: The Pragmatic Roots.**  CMK is a *strict superset* of Makefile[^cmk-make], which is itself basically a superset of bash.  It's rough and ready for the everyday dirty work of adhoc-automation, with zero friction in the middle.  DAGs and goals, plain and foundational, but you can start structured and stay structured by "promoting" shell gradually to RealCode(tm) in a way that helps to manage complexity.  "Pragmatic" doesn't mean "dumb" though, and "simple" doesn't mean "cliche".  Everything in the [standard lib]({{_}}/standard-lib) becomes first-class, from the functional-leaning flux[^flux] to the container support and polyglots[^polyglots], all with improved syntax and better reflection.
> {: .cmk-card .cmk-overview }
>
{{ ('> ' ~ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/tier-1.cmk', github=github, collapsed=True)) | replace('\n', '\n> ') }}

<!-- card-break: prevents python-markdown from merging adjacent blockquotes -->

> **Tier-2: A High-level Language & Other Crowd Pleasers.** As a high-level language, CMK is *almost* a python[^python] in many ways and is *definitely* a lisp[^lisp].  What does that mean?  Well, you probably **already speak cmk-lang** per the last section, but it's also **much bigger** than you think.  Freely mix in things like docstrings, [callforms]({{_}}/cmk/concepts/#callforms) and namespacing if you want it, but when you outgrow "organized scripting", you can reach for more structured programming, including things like *classes, metaclasses, and actors*.  The result: less technical friction crossing data/runtime boundaries, *and* less cognitive friction crossing task-complexity boundaries.  Slightly further downstream: a practical work bench for tool-wrappers and first-class DSLs.
> {: .cmk-card .cmk-overview }
>
{{ ('> ' ~ site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/tier-2.cmk', github=github, collapsed=True)) | replace('\n', '\n> ') }}

<!-- card-break: prevents python-markdown from merging adjacent blockquotes -->

> **Tier-3: Nerd-Sniping the Big-Brains.**  This isn't about first-class support for polyglots[^polyglots] (which are actually practical), or even about hitting you up for fun with theorems and quines.[^theorems]  There are several powerful *algebras* that you might be interested in, which operate over language primitives like targets, ambients, callform-channels, and banana-brackets.  CMK is practical, but also the **weirdest thing you'll see today**, and there's a fractal quality to that.  It has a [certain diverse pedigree]({{_}}/cmk/paradigm) of influences, and richly expressive [technical foundations]({{_}}/cmk/paradigm#language-properties) that are carefully designed.  But on the other hand, and at first glance.. things like the *compiler, runtime, and concurrency story* might seem [barking mad]({{_}}/runtime) in many respects.  Nevertheless, CMK is bootstrapped, self-hosted, and even self-modifying in most of the ways you can probably think of, and *it's got all that metacircular goodness that you crave!*  Including a multistage compiler, an interpreter, a repl and frame-level debugger, and a very strange VM.  Besides bringing support for things like coroutines and swarms, there are weirder consequences.  Goal-resolution itself becomes *just another protocol* which extensions can modify, and since this first-classes the DAG flow, it enables things like backtracking.
> {: .cmk-card .cmk-overview }
>
